<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<AccessEntity continueOnError="true" enabled="true" name="AE-Resolve-App">
    <DisplayName>AE-Resolve-App</DisplayName>
    <!-- Recover the caller's name when VerifyAPIKey refused before publishing it.
         A key that is valid but not scoped for the requested resource fails with
         InvalidApiKeyForGivenResource, and Apigee raises that from inside the
         policy: a debug session over the whole transaction shows developer.app.name,
         verifyapikey.VA-VerifyAPIKey.app.name and every other identity variable
         unset, and the app name appearing nowhere at all. The record then said
         "unauthenticated" for a caller who was perfectly well identified and merely
         over-reaching, which is the opposite of what happened and the exact record
         an investigation cares most about.

         Looking the app up by consumer key is independent of VerifyAPIKey and so
         survives its failure. It resolves the credential, not the authorization:
         a name here says only "this request carried the credential registered to
         that app", and the outcome field alongside it still says denied. An
         unregistered key resolves to nothing and the record correctly stays
         unauthenticated.

         continueOnError, because this runs inside FaultRules: a policy error here
         would abandon the rest of the rule and take the audit record with it, so
         a failed lookup must cost the record a field rather than the whole line.

         The policy's output holds the app's credentials as well as its name. It is
         never logged; only the name is lifted out of it, by EV-App-Name. -->
    <EntityType value="app"/>
    <EntityIdentifier ref="request.header.x-api-key" type="consumerkey"/>
</AccessEntity>
